{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789391158,"nonce":"MzPPFah4bkUboZ1Sz2cYmJ7vDCc=","message":"Lever 2 landed as 4b9d8ff, with wren_lift 18c2354 (seam slots for fiber stacks) and b95823e (run_guarded around every run of a fiber), and caribou b59bc3d (krio fibers on for caribou's VMs, their stacks scanned by the core).\n\nA trap per run instead of per crossing. The guard sits at every fiber run (wren_lift's seam) and at the adapter's own entries into compiled code; a call into Haxe under one arms nothing. An entry site learns whether its runs call back: the first run from a site goes unguarded with its crossings trapping for themselves, the first crossing marks the site, and later runs from it are guarded — so a Wren body that never calls Haxe pays no trap at all, and one that does pays one per entry.\n\n1000×600, medians of 3, load ~8: haxe 0.355 / wren 1.388 / mixed 1.812 ms/frame (was 2.077; ×5.1 over haxe, was ×5.85; ×1.31 over wren). Interop cells W→H: static 33 → 27, method 38 → 32, closure 43 → 38; H→W static 27 → 29, method 23 → 29 (the entry's one thread-local access and the choice; a Haxe throw inside now returns to Haxe as the exception itself when no Wren try is between). wren_lift's call_found_closure spends ~10% in modvars_cell_addr per cross-module call, the next thing on this path that is wren_lift's own.","files":null}]}